# Drawing a Curve with pgfplots from a Data File

Here are several ways to plot data from a file where only y-values are provided (one per line) and x-values auto-increment.

## Method 1: Using `x expr=\coordindex`

This is the simplest approach - it uses the line index as the x-coordinate.

```latex
\documentclass{standalone}
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}

\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}
    \addplot table [
        x expr=\coordindex,  % x = 0, 1, 2, 3, ...
        y index=0            % y values from first (only) column
    ] {data.txt};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

## Method 2: Starting x from 1 instead of 0

```latex
\addplot table [
    x expr=\coordindex+1,  % x = 1, 2, 3, 4, ...
    y index=0
] {data.txt};
```

## Method 3: Custom x increment (e.g., 0.5)

```latex
\addplot table [
    x expr=\coordindex*0.5,  % x = 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, ...
    y index=0
] {data.txt};
```

## Example Data File (`data.txt`)

```
2.5
3.1
4.7
5.2
6.8
7.3
8.1
```

## Complete Working Example

```latex
\documentclass{standalone}
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}

\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
    xlabel={Index},
    ylabel={Value},
    title={Data from file},
    grid=major
]
    \addplot[blue, thick, mark=*] table [
        x expr=\coordindex,
        y index=0
    ] {data.txt};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

## Key Points

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `x expr=\coordindex` | Uses row number (0-based) as x |
| `y index=0` | Reads y from the first column |
| `\coordindex+1` | Makes x start from 1 |
| `\coordindex*step` | Custom spacing between x values |

